安德鲁对他们对他的家人下手感到难过，但他不会让他们击垮自己。格蕾丝·马拉德从未动摇过她儿子是无辜的信念。她向任何愿意倾听的人讲述他的案件。其中一个人是科琳·伊根（Colleen Egan），她当时在第7频道的时事新闻节目《今日今晚》（Today Tonight）工作。科琳对《今日今晚》的报道不感兴趣，但她个人很感兴趣。引起她注意的是一位知名大律师马尔科姆·麦卡斯克（Malcolm McCusker）写的一封信。科琳曾经是一名法庭记者，所以她知道法律圈里谁是谁。

66
00:44:23.000 --> 00:45:11.000
McCusker was well respected, and he had written that Andrew's case was very unsettling. But there was nothing that could be done unless they came up with fresh evidence that if presented to a jury, may change the outcome. That was enough to interest Colleen to take a look at the court transcripts. After which she was convinced Andrew had been given a raw deal. When Grace and Jackie went to tell Andrew that a reporter was interested in helping them, Andrew was far from impressed. Convinced this was just another part of the conspiracy. He refused to talk and said he didn't want any help. Colleen advised Grace and Jackie that they should first worry about getting Andrew's psychiatric help before they worried about fighting the case.
麦卡斯克备受尊敬，他写道安德鲁的案件非常令人不安。但除非他们能拿出新的证据，如果提交给陪审团可能会改变结果，否则无能为力。这足以引起科琳查看法庭记录的兴趣。之后，她确信安德鲁受到了不公正的对待。当格蕾丝和杰基去告诉安德鲁有一位记者有兴趣帮助他们时，安德鲁一点也不觉得印象深刻。他确信这只是阴谋的另一部分。他拒绝交谈，说他不需要任何帮助。科琳建议格蕾丝和杰基，在担心打官司之前，应该先担心为安德鲁寻求精神病治疗。

67
00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:52.000
The prison was initially reluctant to force Andrew into any treatment. Sure, his behaviour was odd, but he wasn't violent, and he wasn't causing trouble. But eventually they changed their stance, and he was sent for a psychiatric assessment. It was being placed in a hospital environment that seemed to snap Andrew back to reality. He realised there was no way his fellow patients were actors, they were legitimate patients. He was in a real hospital. It suddenly became clear to Andrew, this was real. It wasn't a conspiracy, he wasn't in a brainwashing facility.
监狱最初不愿强迫安德鲁接受任何治疗。当然，他的行为很奇怪，但他并不暴力，也没有惹麻烦。但最终他们改变了立场，他被送去进行精神评估。正是被安置在医院环境中，似乎让安德鲁回到了现实。他意识到他的病友不可能是演员，他们是合法的病人。他是在一家真正的医院里。安德鲁突然清楚地意识到，这是真实的。这不是阴谋，他不在洗脑设施里。

68
00:45:52.000 --> 00:46:35.000
The switch was flicked. Andrew started to come back. Gone were his delusional ramblings. Andrew wanted to cooperate with the psychiatrist and prove he was innocent. After a few months of treatment, his condition had greatly improved and he was sent back to prison. Andrew apologised to his mum and to his sister, saying that conspiracy brainwashing theory was the only way he could rationalise what had happened to him. Jackie called Colleen and told her the good news. Andrew was ready. It was a long process.
开关被拨动了。安德鲁开始回归。他那些妄想的胡言乱语消失了。安德鲁想与精神科医生合作，证明自己是无辜的。经过几个月的治疗，他的病情大为好转，被送回了监狱。安德鲁向他的母亲和妹妹道歉，说那种阴谋洗脑理论是他唯一能用来合理化发生在他身上事情的方法。杰基打电话给科琳，告诉她这个好消息。安德鲁准备好了。这是一个漫长的过程。

69
00:46:35.000 --> 00:47:16.000
Colleen worked on the case for four years with the help of Andrew's barrister and many others. But they couldn't find what they needed. They hadn't uncovered any fresh evidence so there was no grounds for a new appeal. Colleen decided to approach an unlikely ally. John Quigley was a well-known lawyer who spent 25 years working with the police union, defending police officers accused of wrongdoing. He rarely lost a case. His reputation was exceptional, known as one of the best lawyers you could get. He was also a big personality who loved the law night and loved the camera.
科琳在安德鲁的律师和许多其他人的帮助下，为这个案子工作了四年。但他们找不到他们需要的东西。他们没有发现任何新的证据，所以没有理由进行新的上诉。科琳决定接触一个不太可能的盟友。约翰·奎格利（John Quigley）是一位知名律师，他花了25年时间与警察工会合作，为被指控不当行为的警官辩护。他很少输掉官司。他的声誉非常出色，被认为是你能找到的最好的律师之一。他也是一个热爱法律、热爱镜头的大人物。

70
00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:54.000
Colleen knew this from her crime reporting days. But Quigley had recently quit law and had taken up politics, becoming a labour party backbencher. During the four years she had been working on the case, Colleen had changed jobs. It just so happened she was now working for a newspaper covering politics. Quigley knew everything there was to know about police procedures and practices. It's what made him such a good person to approach. But it was risky. 25 years of defending police officers had developed some solid relationships and contacts.
科琳从她做犯罪报道的日子里就知道这一点。但奎格利最近辞去了律师职务，投身政界，成为工党的后座议员。在她为这个案子工作的四年里，科琳换了工作。碰巧她现在在一家报社负责报道政治。奎格利对警察程序和做法了如指掌。这使他成为一个非常好的接触对象。但这很冒险。25年为警官辩护的经历让他建立了一些稳固的关系和人脉。

71
00:47:54.000 --> 00:48:31.000
He was the only non-police officer to be given a life membership of the police union. Colleen approached him and let him know she strongly believed there was an innocent man behind bars. What better way to start a career in politics? Helping get an innocent man out of prison. Quigley didn't make any promises but he told Colleen he would have a look at the case and get back to her. It didn't take him long to find a problem. It was what happened after the first interview when Andrew was charged with assaulting police. Andrew was homeless at the time.
他是唯一一个被授予警察工会终身会员资格的非警务人员。科琳接触了他，让他知道她坚信有一个无辜的人被关在监狱里。还有什么比这更好的方式来开启政治生涯呢？帮助一个无辜的人出狱。奎格利没有做出任何承诺，但他告诉科琳他会看一看这个案子，然后再给她答复。他没花多久就发现了一个问题。那是第一次审讯后，安德鲁被指控袭击警察时发生的事情。安德鲁当时无家可归。

72
00:48:31.000 --> 00:49:06.000
It was very unusual for somebody to be released on bail if they had nowhere to go. He compared this fact with the evidence of Detective Brandon, who arrested Andrew on the warrant a week later and conducted the second interview. Brandon said he was just driving around and by chance happened to see Andrew in the park. So he arrested him on the warrant. Quigley was able to put it together pretty quickly. He realized Andrew was released because they were running an undercover operation and Brandon didn't just happen to drive past him in the park.
如果一个人无处可去，被保释是非常不寻常的。他将这一事实与布兰登（Brandon）警探的证据进行了比较，后者在一周后根据逮捕令逮捕了安德鲁并进行了第二次审讯。布兰登说他只是开车到处转转，偶然在公园里看到了安德鲁。所以他根据逮捕令逮捕了他。奎格利很快就把事情拼凑起来了。他意识到安德鲁被释放是因为他们正在进行卧底行动，而布兰登并不是偶然开车经过公园。

73
00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:44.000
They knew where he was the entire time. It was unusual that there was an undercover operation and it wasn't mentioned in the trial at all. The approach to Quigley had paid off. These were things that stood out to him that the others just didn't see. And Quigley assumed that there was probably more that had been hidden. He decided to meet personally with the forensic pathologist Dr Cook. Quigley asked him how it came to be that a wrench was determined to be the murder weapon. Dr Cook said he had no idea.
他们一直都知道他在哪里。不寻常的是，有一个卧底行动，但在庭审中根本没有被提及。接触奎格利得到了回报。这些是其他人没有注意到的突出问题。奎格利推测可能还有更多被隐藏的东西。他决定亲自会见法医病理学家库克（Cook）医生。奎格利问他是如何确定扳手是凶器的。库克医生说他不知道。

74
00:49:44.000 --> 00:50:21.000
He told Quigley the same thing he says he told the detectives in charge of the case and the prosecutor. I find it hard to believe a wrench could have caused those injuries. Quigley showed him the sketch that Andrew had drawn of the Sidcrome wrench. Dr Cook had never seen it before. This was enough for the ball to start rolling. Quigley used his political connections to be given the prosecution file. All they had at that point were the court transcripts. It surprised them to say that tests had been done back in 1994.
他告诉奎格利他告诉负责此案的警探和检察官的同样的话：“我很难相信扳手会造成这些伤害。”奎格利向他展示了安德鲁画的Sidcrome扳手的草图。库克医生以前从未见过。这足以让事情开始运转。奎格利利用他的政治关系获得了检方档案。当时他们手里只有法庭记录。令他们惊讶的是，1994年就已经进行了测试。

75
00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:57.000
To see what wounds a wrench made on a pig's head. The wounds were different to the wounds suffered by Pium. This test was never disclosed to the defence or brought up at the trial. The prosecution file also confirmed what Quigley had already worked out anyway. That there was an undercover operation. Several years had now passed since the trial. The prosecutor, Cam Bates, who was in one of his first high profile cases, was now a highly respected prosecutor with a good reputation.
看看扳手在猪头上造成了什么伤口。伤口与PM遭受的伤口不同。这个测试从未向辩方披露，也没有在庭审中提出。检方档案也证实了奎格利已经推断出的事实：确实有卧底行动。距离庭审已经过去了好几年。检察官卡姆·贝茨（Cam Bates）当时处理的是他首批高调案件之一，现在已是一位声誉卓著、备受尊敬的检察官。

76
00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:36.000
John McKechnie, who was the top prosecutor in the state at the time and who gave the detectives the go ahead to charge, was now a serving Supreme Court judge. Detective Sherville and Cape Horn were now assistant police commissioners. Detective Brandon, who arrested Andrew and conducted the second interview, was now a superintendent. Not surprisingly, there was resistance to what Quigley was saying. Headlines read that Quigley was trying to get a vicious murderer off the hook on a non-disclosure technicality. Officers in charge of the case and the prosecutor's office
当时该州最高检察官、批准警探进行指控的约翰·麦基奇尼（John McKechnie），现在是一名在职最高法院法官。谢维尔（Sherville）和卡佩尔（Cape Horn）警探现在是助理警察局长。逮捕安德鲁并进行第二次审讯的布兰登警探现在是一名警司。不出所料，奎格利所说的话遭到了抵制。头条新闻称，奎格利正试图利用非披露的技术细节让一个恶毒的杀人犯逃脱惩罚。负责此案的警官和检察官办公室

77
00:51:36.000 --> 00:52:14.000
maintained that the case against Andrew was strong. He had made a solid confession and a jury was convinced beyond a reasonable doubt he was the killer. But enough had now been uncovered for the case to be referred back to the court of criminal appeal. The prosecution was determined to fight the appeal. They conceded that, yes, they should have disclosed all of the information, but they maintained it was just an oversight. And it wouldn't have affected the outcome of the trial. Well-known barrister, Malcolm McCusker, whose letter got Colleen interested in the case, was now defending Andrew.
坚称针对安德鲁的案件证据确凿。他做出了坚实的供词，陪审团确信他就是凶手，排除合理怀疑。但现在已经揭露了足够多的信息，案件被移交回刑事上诉法院。检方决心反击上诉。他们承认，是的，他们应该披露所有信息，但他们坚称这只是疏忽。而且这不会影响庭审结果。那封让科琳对案件产生兴趣的信的作者、知名大律师马尔科姆·麦卡斯克，现在正在为安德鲁辩护。

78
00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:51.000
And because the case was back before the court of criminal appeal and full disclosure had been made, there were a few more issues that came up. Michelle, the girl Andrew was living with at the time of the murder, was made to give several charges against him. What she was living with at the time of the murder was made to give several different statements to police. Each one slightly different, adding or taking out little details to better fit the theory that Andrew was responsible. Only her last statement was given to the defense. Catherine Barsden, the 13-year-old witness who saw the suspicious man in the shop,
由于案件回到了刑事上诉法院，并且进行了全面披露，出现了一些更多的问题。米歇尔（Michelle），安德鲁在谋杀案发生时住在一起的女孩，被迫对他提出了几项指控。她在谋杀案发生时住在一起的人被迫向警方提供了几份不同的陈述。每一份都略有不同，增加或删减了一些小细节，以更好地符合安德鲁是责任人的理论。只有她最后一份陈述被提供给了辩方。凯瑟琳·巴斯登，那个在店里看到可疑男子的13岁证人，

79
00:52:51.000 --> 00:53:30.000
also made a sketch of the man she saw as soon as she got home. And she wrote a statement out herself, different to what was later presented at court. Catherine had also failed to pick Andrew out of a photo line-up, and she didn't identify him in court as the man she saw. It was found that a forensic report had also been amended. The amended version was the one supplied to the defense and presented as evidence at the trial. It was amended to remove the part that said there was no trace of salt water on Andrew's clothing. If you remember back to Andrew's theory on the crime,
一回家也画了她看到的男子的草图。她自己写了一份陈述，与后来在法庭上提交的陈述不同。凯瑟琳也没能从照片排队中认出安德鲁，她在法庭上也没有认出他就是她看到的那个男人。人们发现一份法医报告也被修改了。修改后的版本是提供给辩方并作为庭审证据提交的版本。它被修改以删除说安德鲁的衣服上没有盐水痕迹的部分。如果你还记得安德鲁对犯罪的理论，

80
00:53:30.000 --> 00:54:17.000
he said that the killer washed his clothes in the salt water because it fucks with the forensics. As they were relying on his third person rinse as a confession, it didn't look good if there was no blood and no traces of salt water on his clothes. But if there was no mention of the salt water not being present, Bates could still give that theory to the jury. Andrew washed his clothes in the river, that's why there was no blood. So Andrew's defense team were arguing that evidence was withheld and statements were changed in order to make the case look a whole lot stronger than what it was. The case was heard in the court of criminal appeal before three Supreme Court judges.
他说凶手在盐水中洗了衣服，因为这会干扰法医鉴定。由于他们依赖他的第三人称洗涤作为供词，如果他的衣服上没有血迹和盐水痕迹，看起来就不太好。但如果没有提到盐水不存在，贝茨仍然可以向陪审团提出那个理论。安德鲁在河里洗了衣服，这就是为什么没有血迹。所以安德鲁的辩护团队认为，为了让案件看起来比实际情况强得多，证据被扣留，陈述被更改。案件在刑事上诉法院由三名最高法院法官审理。

81
00:54:17.000 --> 00:55:05.000
On the 3rd of December 2003, they made their ruling, which was again unanimous. Appeal dismissed, conviction upheld. To keep it simple, basically they agreed with the prosecution, yes there were clearly nondisclosure issues, but no, those nondisclosures wouldn't have affected the outcome of the trial. Neither Sherville or Cape Horn were called as witnesses by the prosecution in the appeal, meaning that offence could not cross-examine them. On the 27th of October 2004, Andrew's defense team presented their case to the High Court.
2003年12月3日，他们做出了裁决，再次一致通过。上诉驳回，定罪维持。简单来说，他们基本上同意检方的观点，是的，确实存在明显的非披露问题，但不，这些非披露不会影响庭审结果。谢维尔或卡佩尔都没有被检方传唤为上诉证人，这意味着辩方无法盘问他们。2004年10月27日，安德鲁的辩护团队向高等法院提交了他们的案件。

82
00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:36.000
Again, it was initially like an application process. This time they were successful. The judges granted special leave for the High Court to hear the appeal, but there was still a long road ahead. All that meant was that they got their foot in the door. It would be another year before the case was actually heard, but their day in the High Court finally came and five judges sat on the panel to hear the appeal.
同样，这最初就像一个申请程序。这一次他们成功了。法官批准了高等法院审理上诉的特别许可，但前方仍有漫长的道路。这意味着他们迈出了第一步。案件实际审理还需要一年时间，但他们在高等法院的日子终于到了，五名法官坐在小组中审理上诉。

83
00:55:36.000 --> 00:56:10.000
After considering all the evidence and all the issues around the case, they reached a decision. And again, it was unanimous. Conviction quashed, a retrial was ordered. They believed there had been a miscarriage of justice. Shortly after the High Court ruling, the Corruption and Crime Commission started investigating allegations of misconduct by the lead detectives and the prosecutor.
在考虑了所有证据和案件周围的所有问题后，他们做出了决定。再次一致通过。定罪撤销，下令重审。他们认为存在司法不公。高等法院裁决后不久，腐败与犯罪委员会开始调查首席警探和检察官的不当行为指控。

84
00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:45.000
The prosecution initially didn't back down, declaring they had every intention of pursuing the retrial. But their attitude changed when a judge ruled that those unrecorded and unsigned confessions wouldn't be admissible in the second trial. With that information in mind, the prosecution elected not to pursue a retrial. They took a parting shot, saying the only reason they weren't pursuing the case is because vital evidence was ruled inadmissible and that Andrew remained their prime suspect.
检方最初没有退缩，宣称他们完全打算进行重审。但当法官裁定那些未录音和未签名的供词在第二次审判中不可采信时，他们的态度改变了。考虑到这一信息，检方选择不进行重审。他们临走前放了一句狠话，说他们不追究此案的唯一原因是关键证据被裁定不可采信，安德鲁仍然是他们的头号嫌疑人。

85
00:56:45.000 --> 00:57:25.000
The police agreed, saying the case would remain closed. Andrew was their man, but he had just been let off on a technicality. After being locked up for 12 years, Andrew was free. Just after Andrew's release, a blood splatter expert was in Perth testifying in an unrelated trial. He studied the photos of bloody clothing that had been taken after PM's murder. Based on those photos, he formed an opinion on who killed PM. His report wasn't good news for PM's husband, Peter Lawrence.
警方表示同意，称案件将保持结案状态。安德鲁是他们的人，但他只是因为技术细节被放走了。被关押12年后，安德鲁自由了。安德鲁获释后不久，一名血迹喷溅专家在珀斯为一起无关的审判作证。他研究了PM谋杀案后拍摄的血衣照片。根据这些照片，他形成了关于谁杀害了PM的观点。他的报告对PM的丈夫彼得·劳伦斯来说不是好消息。

86
00:57:25.000 --> 00:58:00.000
Despite the police saying the case would remain closed, public pressure mounted and they were forced to reopen it and have another look. They formed a squad to re-examine every piece of evidence in the case. New technology was now available that didn't exist 12 years earlier and it was hoped that this new technology might help uncover new information. And it did. A partial palm print was fed through the computer system and it quickly produced a match. In July 1994, just seven weeks after PM's murder,
尽管警方表示案件将保持结案，但公众压力越来越大，他们被迫重新开启案件并再次查看。他们成立了一个小组，重新审查案件中的每一件证据。现在有了12年前不存在的新技术，人们希望这项新技术可能有助于发现新信息。确实如此。一个部分掌纹被输入计算机系统，很快就产生了匹配。1994年7月，在PM谋杀案发生仅七周后，

87
00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:42.000
an English backpacker, Simon Rockford, was arrested for the murder of his girlfriend, Brigida Dickens. Simon had been in Perth for only six months at the time. Brigida was the local he met when he arrived to you. The cause of her death was traumatic brain injury. Simon bludgeoned her to death with a weapon he had made himself using an old weight and an old handle. Simon had spun a whole heap of lies to Brigida about who he really was and what he was doing. He gave grand tales of being rich and successful, when really he was a criminal who had overstayed his tourist visa.
一名英国背包客西蒙·罗克福德（Simon Rockford）因谋杀他的女友布里吉达·狄更斯（Brigida Dickens）而被捕。西蒙当时在珀斯仅待了六个月。布里吉达是他到达时遇到的当地人。她死亡的原因是创伤性脑损伤。西蒙用他自己用旧重物和旧把手制作的武器将她殴打致死。西蒙对布里吉达撒了一大堆谎，关于他是谁以及他在做什么。他吹嘘自己富有且成功，而实际上他是一个签证过期的罪犯。

88
